  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM EDT Wed May 13 2026\n\nA surface low continues to lift northeast, now over the eastern \nshore of Lake Huron. Cyclonic flow off Lake Michigan has resulted in \nmostly cloudy skies through late morning but this is now clearing as \nhigh pressure centered over eastern Nebraska exerts its influence. \nCold tonight, but shy of frost/freeze concern as a steady northwest \nwind near 5 to 10 mph keeps temperatures right near 40 degrees. \n\nHigh pressure remain in control Thursday allowing highs in the 60s \nwhich is still shy of the normal high near 70. A weak upper-level \ndisturbance limps in from the west Friday and presents a 20-40% \nchance of showers mainly south of US 30. Preceding high pressure and \ndry air is a limiting factor along with modest forcing overall. Look \nfor a greater coverage of showers and thunderstorms, though not a \nwashout, Saturday and Sunday. With favorable moisture flux from the \nGulf along the western edge of a subtropical ridge, an orphaned \nfront from a departing Canadian low lingers over the Midwest and \nbecomes the focus for scattered showers and storms at times.  \n\nThe aformentioned ridge is maximized over the Appalachians Monday \nresulting in highs well into the 80s. Cannot rule out a 90-degree \nreading, but the general concensus from a number of NWS offices was \nto lower high temperatures Monday away from 90 degrees, noting a 4-\ndegree bias correction. Nonetheless, becoming very warm this weekend \nthrough Monday. Cooler and stormy Monday night through Tuesday as a \ncold front moves through. SPC day 6 outlook threatens to creep east \ninto northwest Indiana through time with marginal severe weather \ningredients noted.",
